<h2>The statutory chain: Sections 15, 16, 17 and 18</h2>
<p>The delayed-payment scheme of the MSMED Act is a chain of four links. Section 15 fixes the buyer&rsquo;s obligation: payment on or before the agreed date, and in no case beyond forty-five days from the day of acceptance or deemed acceptance. Section 16 attaches the sanction: on failure, compound interest with monthly rests at three times the bank rate notified by the Reserve Bank of India, running from the appointed day or the agreed date. Section 17 declares what the buyer is liable to pay — the amount with the interest thereon. Section 18 then opens the forum: any party to a dispute may make a reference to the Micro and Small Enterprises Facilitation Council <em>with regard to any amount due under Section 17</em>.</p>
<p>Those last words are the jurisdictional anchor. The Council&rsquo;s process — conciliation first, and failing settlement, arbitration under Section 18(3) as if under an arbitration agreement, with a ninety-day target in Section 18(5) — exists to quantify and recover the price of what was actually supplied or rendered, plus the statutory interest. It is a recovery engine, not a general commercial tribunal.</p>

<h2>Inside and outside the basket</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Inside: unpaid price.</strong> Invoices for goods delivered and accepted, or services rendered, within the supplier-buyer relationship the Act covers.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Inside: Section 16 interest.</strong> The compounded statutory interest — claimable even where the principal has since been paid late.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Outside: damages at large.</strong> Lost profit on cancelled or unexecuted orders, idle machinery, inventory bought in anticipation, reputational loss — breach claims, not amounts due for supplies made.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Outside: non-monetary reliefs.</strong> Specific performance of a purchase order, injunctions, declarations about the contract&rsquo;s validity — nothing in Sections 15 to 18 confers such powers.</div>
</div>
<p>The line is functional, not formal. If the claim answers the question &ldquo;what remains unpaid for what I supplied?&rdquo;, it belongs in the reference. If it answers &ldquo;what did the buyer&rsquo;s conduct cost my business?&rdquo;, it is a damages claim for a civil or commercial suit, or for arbitration under the contract&rsquo;s own clause where one exists.</p>

<h2>The buyer&rsquo;s side of the same line</h2>
<p>None of this leaves the buyer defenceless inside the reference. Disputes about whether goods were supplied, whether they conformed to specification, and how much is genuinely due are precisely what the Section 18 process adjudicates; the buyer&rsquo;s quality and quantity objections operate within it, and counter-claims by the buyer have been held maintainable in the statutory arbitration. What the buyer cannot achieve is escape: Section 24 gives Sections 15 to 23 overriding effect over anything inconsistent in any other law, and a challenge to the eventual award requires the deposit of seventy-five per cent of the awarded amount under Section 19 before it will even be entertained.</p>

<h2>Drafting the reference strategically</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Plead the supply, not the grievance: anchor every head of claim to identified invoices, delivery challans, acceptance and the Section 15 due dates. The interest computation should be annexed, compounded monthly at three times the notified bank rate.</p>
  <p>Strip out damages heads before filing. Claims that do not belong weaken the ones that do, and give the buyer its best argument on a Section 19 challenge.</p>
  <p>Run parallel tracks consciously: a Samadhaan reference for the amounts due, and — where real breach losses exist — a separate suit or contractual arbitration for damages, each pleaded consistently with the other.</p>
  <p>Preserve the record of acceptance: deemed acceptance after fifteen days without written objection is frequently the decisive fact in these references.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article explains the general scope of Facilitation Council references under the MSMED Act for information. Forum strategy in any actual dispute depends on the contract, the documents and the state of the account between the parties.</div>
